╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ minimal_edit_persona_short                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ **What the failures showed**                                                              │
│                          │ - **Empty outputs grew with prompt length.** Empty outputs (budget burned on reasoning)   │
│                          │ went from 3–4 with short prompts to 8 with the long checklist prompt. Long rule lists     │
│                          │ make the model audit its draft against every rule while thinking.                         │
│                          │ - **The only pass was a rewrite.** It kept the source's original human voice almost       │
│                          │ untouched.                                                                                │
│                          │ - **Fluent explanation gets flagged.** Every flagged answer was a tidy, evenly paced      │
│                          │ explainer, even when it was sprinkled with "honestly".                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ **The new approach**                                                                      │
│                          │ 1. **One short persona instead of a checklist.** There are fewer items to verify, so the  │
│                          │ model has less to re-check while thinking.                                                │
│                          │ 2. **An explicit thinking cap with a reason.** The prompt says rules are not to be        │
│                          │ checked one by one, and that the first natural wording is the wanted output.              │
│                          │ 3. **Rewrites as minimal-edit copy-editing.** The model keeps most of the source's own    │
│                          │ sentences, quirks and order, and only smooths the wording where asked. This preserves the │
│                          │ human signal the detector rewards and also stops sentence-by-sentence planning.           │
│                          │ 4. **Answers, notes and posts in a real forum-comment register.** That means:             │
│                          │    - a blunt opinion first;                                                               │
│                          │    - uneven, slightly messy sentences, with an occasional lowercase casual fragment;      │
│                          │    - talking about one angle rather than an ordered tour of the mechanism;                │
│                          │    - comparing with what "most people" think;                                             │
│                          │    - mild uncertainty.                                                                    │
│                          │ 5. **No invention of facts.** The prompt still forbids made-up facts and keeps length     │
│                          │ targets.                                                                                  │
